### 2\. Install both on Ubuntu

First update Ubuntu:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

Install Ettercap:

```bash
sudo apt install ettercap-text-only -y
```

Install Bettercap:

```bash
sudo apt install bettercap -y
```

Verify installations:

```bash
ettercap --version
bettercap --version
```

Check their help menus:

```bash
ettercap --help
bettercap -h
```

### 3\. Ubuntu network preparation

Before using either tool, identify your network interface.

```bash
ip -br addr
```

Check your gateway:

```bash
ip route
```

Check your interface details:

```bash
ip link show
```

Check your ARP/neighbour table:

```bash
ip neigh show
```

Check whether packet capture is working:

```bash
sudo tcpdump -D
```

Replace `eth0` in the examples below with your actual interface, such as `ens33`, `enp0s3` or `wlan0`.

### 4\. Ettercap command reference

Ettercap supports text, curses and graphical interfaces. Its command-line options are documented in its manual. ettercap-project.org +1

A. Basic commands

```bash
ettercap -h
ettercap -v
ettercap -T
ettercap -C
ettercap -G
```

B. Interface and traffic inspection

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -p
```

This starts text-mode sniffing without enabling promiscuous mode.

Read an existing packet capture:

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -r capture.pcap
```

Save an authorized capture:

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -w capture.pcap
```

Use a protocol filter:

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0 -t arp
```

C. Plugin discovery

```bash
ettercap -P list
```

Run a selected plugin:

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -P PLUGIN_NAME
```

Replace `PLUGIN_NAME` with an actual plugin from the list and read its documentation before enabling it.

D. Host and traffic analysis

```bash
sudo ettercap -T -i eth0
```

Use the interactive interface to inspect discovered hosts and available protocol information.

E. Offline capture analysis

```bash
etterlog -h
```

```bash
etterlog -r capture.pcap
```

Check the exact input options with:

```bash
man etterlog
```

### 5\. Bettercap command reference

Bettercap uses an interactive console and modular architecture. You can execute multiple commands in one session. bettercap +1

A. Start Bettercap

```bash
sudo bettercap
```

Specify your interface:

```bash
sudo bettercap -iface eth0
```

Display version:

```bash
bettercap -version
```

B. Interactive console commands

Inside Bettercap:

```text
help
help MODULE
get
get net.recon
get net.probe
net.show
clear
quit
```

C. Network reconnaissance

Start passive network reconnaissance:

```text
net.recon on
```

Display discovered devices:

```text
net.show
```

Stop reconnaissance:

```text
net.recon off
```

D. Network probing

On an isolated network that you own or have permission to test:

```text
net.probe on
```

Stop probing:

```text
net.probe off
```

E. Event monitoring

```text
events.stream on
```

Stop event monitoring:

```text
events.stream off
```

F. Session configuration

```text
help
get
set
```

You can also start Bettercap with a command:

```bash
sudo bettercap -iface eth0 -eval "net.recon on; net.show"
```

G. Caplets

Caplets are scripts that automate Bettercap sessions.

```bash
bettercap -h
```

List available caplets:

```bash
bettercap -caplets-path
```

For a specific caplet directory, inspect its files:

```bash
find /usr/share/bettercap -type f -name '*.cap'
```

### 6\. Where the critical vulnerabilities are hiding

This is the part I particularly want you to understand.

Ettercap and Bettercap are primarily network-security testing tools. They do not automatically discover critical application vulnerabilities.

For deeper bug bounty research, investigate these areas:

| Research area        | What to investigate                                  |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| ARP security         | ARP spoofing protections and network isolation       |
| DNS security         | DNS trust, validation and cache poisoning resistance |
| TLS security         | Certificate validation and downgrade weaknesses      |
| HTTP security        | Proxy trust and request interpretation               |
| WebSocket            | Authentication and authorization                     |
| Network segmentation | Unauthorized communication between network zones     |
| Session security     | Session exposure and invalidation                    |
| API security         | Authentication and business-logic weaknesses         |
| Protocol parsing     | Inconsistent interpretation of network messages      |
| IPv6                 | Router advertisements and network trust              |

One particularly interesting research direction: inconsistent interpretation of the same traffic by different components.

For example:

Client sends request

Proxy interprets request

Backend interprets request differently

Potential security boundary failure

This is one reason to investigate HTTP request smuggling, proxy desynchronization, cache poisoning and authentication inconsistencies.

